 0)\"/>\n      <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-1\" transform=\"translate(606.09375 0)\"/>\n      <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-11\" transform=\"translate(659.5 0)\"/>\n      <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5\" transform=\"translate(716.296875 0)\"/>\n     </g>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"pcbda7629ed\">\n   <rect x=\"19.6\" y=\"28\" width=\"279\" height=\"127.512\"/>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · Saudi dairy capacity vs demand, billion litres (case data)","note":"Includes competitors' announced expansions, but not Desert Dairy's new plant."},"answer":"<p>Industry capacity grows 25% while demand grows ~23%. Utilisation is ~87% today and stays ~85% in three years even before our plant. Adding 0.2bn more would push it to ~81%. <b>So-what:</b> more capacity → pressure on prices. Model a lower contribution (e.g. $0.25/litre) for the new plant's volume, and consider building it in phases.</p>"}],"reco":"Do the $20M upgrade immediately: it pays back in under 3 years and covers demand until year 3. Approve the new plant now because of the 3-year build, but in two phases (e.g. 100M litres first), because competitors are also expanding and prices may come under pressure. Use co-packers to cover any short-term peaks. Next steps: firm up raw-milk supply contracts, and rerun the plant economics at $0.25/litre.","great":["Builds the demand table year by year","Spots that the 3-year build forces the decision now","Tries the cheaper option first","Considers competitor supply and prices, not only our own demand"],"id":8,"slug":"desert-dairy"}